Work (functional or purely technical) goes through **PILOT**, a lightweight agent process: each piece of
work is a **workflow**, a data-defined sequence of steps (`python3 .pilot/pilot.py workflow list`, then
`workflow show <id>`), run by a skill in isolated, low-token agent contexts instead of one accumulating one.
The run skills are `/pilot-need` (a request becomes wiki criteria and a story), `/pilot-plan` (a story
becomes ADRs and buildable tickets), `/pilot-build` (a ticket becomes a merged PR), `/pilot-accept` (the Recette
on the deployed main), `/pilot-report` (an abnormal behavior is qualified and routed) and `/pilot-express`
(a simplified path for accounts allowed it). Each is driven on its own; no single command runs them all.
Its skills (`.claude/skills/pilot-*`) and agent personas (`.claude/agents/pilot-*.md`) are copied into this
repo from the `pilot` distribution repo (github.com/Jonas-Eve/pilot), not written here. `.pilot/pilot-process.md`
and `.pilot/pilot-link-runner.md` are verbatim copies of its canonical spec (vocabulary, labels, how a workflow
runs), kept in sync by `/pilot-update`, never hand-edited here. Read them before creating or moving a ticket.

When the project's `wiki` module is enabled (`.pilot/state.json`), `wiki/` is the source of truth for what the
product does and what is promised: read it (`/pilot-wiki <question>`, or `python3 .pilot/pilot.py wiki index --depth 1` then `wiki search`)
before answering about the product, and never contradict it from the code alone.

When the project's `architecture` module is enabled, `architecture/` says how the product is built and why: read
its guidelines (`/pilot-architecture <question>`, or `python3 .pilot/pilot.py arch index --depth 1` then `arch search`) before changing code, and
never break one without a decision record.
